  "pr_number": 13318,
  "issue_number": 13167,
  "issue_title": "Fix C parser reporting the QUERY method as \"<unknown>\"",
  "issue_body": "## What do these changes do?\n\nThe vendored llhttp already recognizes the `QUERY` method ([RFC 10008](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc10008), enum value 46), but `_http_parser.pyx` builds its enum\u2192name table with `METHODS_COUNT = 46` (ids 0\u201345), so `http_method_str(46)` fell back to the literal `\"<unknown>\"`. Servers built with the C extensions therefore answered `405 Method Not Allowed` for routes registered for `QUERY` (the pure-Python parser was unaffected). This PR:\n\n- bumps `METHODS_COUNT` to 47 with a comment documenting the invariant (must span llhttp's `llhttp_method_t`),\n- declares `HTTP_QUERY` in `_cparser.pxd` to keep the enum mirror complete,\n- adds `hdrs.METH_QUERY`,\n- adds `QUERY` to the client's `IDEMPOTENT_METHODS` (the RFC declares it \"explicitly safe and idempotent\"), as suggested in the issue.\n\nDeliberately **not** included: `QUERY` in `hdrs.METH_ALL`. `RouteDef.register()` dispatches `METH_ALL` members to `router.add_<method>()`, so membership requires the full sugar family (`UrlDispatcher.add_query`, `web.query`, `RouteTableDef.query`, and arguably `ClientSession.query`). I can do that as a follow-up if you want it. (While checking this I noticed `CONNECT` and `TRACE` are in `METH_ALL` but have no `add_connect`/`add_trace`, so `app.add_routes([web.route(\"CONNECT\", ...)])` raises `AttributeError` today \u2014 worth a separate issue?)\n\n## Are there changes in behavior for the user?\n\nServers using the C extensions now see `request.method == \"QUERY\"` and route it correctly instead of returning 405. The client treats `QUERY` as idempotent for the closed keep-alive connection retry, like the other RFC-idempotent methods.\n\n## Is it a substantial burden for the maintainers to support this?\n\nNo. The table size is a single constant tied to the vendored llhttp version, and the new comment states the invariant to check when bumping llhttp.\n\n## Related issue number\n\nFixes #13160. The bug also affects 3.14/3.15 (reproduced on the 3.14.1 wheels), so backport labels probably make sense.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] I think the code is well written\n- [x] Unit tests for the changes exist\n- [ ] Documentation reflects the changes \u2014 N/A (`METH_*` constants have no individual docs; no documented behavior changes)\n- [x] If you provide code modification, please add yourself to `CONTRIBUTORS.txt` \u2014 already listed\n- [x] Add a new news fragment into the `CHANGES/` folder\n\n<details>\n<summary>Local test evidence (CPython 3.13, C extensions compiled)</summary>\n\n- `test_parse_query_method`: passes on both `c-parser` and `py-parser`; verified it **fails** against the unfixed parser with `AssertionError: assert '<unknown>' == 'QUERY'` (c-parser only), so it is a true reproducer.\n- `test_query_method` (functional round-trip with a JSON body): passes.\n- `tests/test_http_parser.py` + `tests/test_web_functional.py`: 918 passed, 26 skipped, 3 xfailed, 1 failed \u2014 the failure (`TestDeflateBuffer::test_feed_eof_no_err_brotli`) is environmental (no brotli in the local venv) and fails identically on unmodified `master`.\n- `tests/test_client_session.py`: 97 passed, 1 skipped.\n- `black`/`isort` clean on all touched Python files.\n- The issue's original scenario on this build: catch-all route sees `request.method == 'QUERY'`, a `QUERY`-only route returns `200` (was `'<unknown>'` / `405` on the 3.14.1 wheels).\n\n</details>\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-08-11T15:12:40Z",
